  "plan": {
    "goal": "Perfuse a liver-on-chip so hepatocytes experience the in vivo hepatic sinusoidal wall shear stress (low shear, ~0.05 Pa).",
    "rationale": "Hepatic sinusoids are a low-shear microvascular bed. The registry lists HepG2 physiological shear at 0.05–0.15 Pa; the sinusoidal end is the low end of this range (literature commonly cites ~0.01–0.05 Pa for sinusoids). I target 0.05 Pa as a defensible sinusoidal value. Using a 400×100 µm rectangular channel (typical OOC geometry) and water-like medium viscosity (1e-3 Pa·s), the flow rate for 0.05 Pa is 2 µL/min. This yields Re ≈ 0.13 (deeply laminar), a 24 s residence time, and only 20 Pa pressure drop — all well within microfluidic norms. Seeding primary human hepatocytes at 1e5 cells/cm² over the 0.08 cm² channel floor gives 8000 cells; at 2 µL/min the perfused O2 supply exceeds demand ~21-fold, so no hypoxia. A lower-shear option (0.01 Pa) would require 0.4 µL/min if the user wants the very lowest sinusoidal value.",
    "chip": {
      "width_um": 400.0,
      "height_um": 100.0,
      "length_mm": 20.0,
      "channel_count": 1,
      "material": "PDMS"
    },
